present the state of the youth address for you all tonight Good evening everyone My name is Joseph Lucero I'm a senior at Srian East High School and I've had the opportunity to serve as heads chair on Mayor's Youth Council this evening uh this year Tonight I want to talk a little bit about my experience in Mayor's Youth Council in our capstone project I had no idea what Mayor's Youth Council was to start the year I almost wasn't even in it I missed the application deadline but luckily Brooks who knew my parents reached out and said I'd be a great candidate So I got an interview and I got accepted And I'm really glad that happened because it's probably been one of the most impactful experiences I've had throughout my high school career And

one of the coolest parts was the people Through Mayor's Youth Council I met students from different schools and different parts of the community that I probably would not have met otherwise People from schools I normally would not interact with became some of my friends And it wasn't just meetings either There was always something going on before after or in between I do not think there was a meeting where people didn't say afterwards and talk about school life events or what's happening around town the drama at other high schools It turned into more than just a leadership group It was like a community And I think that says a lot because bringing students together from different backgrounds in schools is not always easy but somehow it worked Another thing that stood out to me was how personal the experience felt The mayor was not just someone who showed up for official events and

disappeared afterwards He took time to talk to us and get to know us and he was genuinely a part of our group One of my favorite memories was our Christmas party It was a fun event and it was one of those nights you remember because everybody was hanging out talking doing the gift exchange sharing cookies and having fun And the mayor was there with us He wasn't there in a formal role He wasn't giving speeches He was just a part of it And little things like that made the experience feel really real He would always keep us updated on his Dualingo streak and how Spanish learning was going which I always thought was pretty funny and

made him feel really relatable Then there was our capstone project We wanted to do something that would directly help people in our community So we created care packages for people experiencing homelessness We put together backpacks filled with thermal base layers toiletries hand sanitizer wipes toothbrushes and other essentials We ended up making 50 backpacks for men and another 50 for women Originally we planned on working with another organization but things changed and we had to adjust We ended up partnering with the Welcome Mat and I honestly think that made the project even better We got to meet them hear about the work they do and it gave us a better understanding of the needs in our community About a week after putting everything together we dropped off the backpacks and

got to see the idea become something real And I think that project represents what May's Youth Council is about It's not just learning about issues it's also about doing something about them This year changed the way I looked at leadership It showed me that leadership is not always standing in front of people Sometimes it's listening Sometimes it's serving Sometimes it's working alongside people and helping where you can And it showed me that students can make a real difference I want to thank the mayor the adviserss Devon Brooks Cotton and everyone who helped with our project And all the students in May's Youth Council Thank you for the friendships the memories the opportunities and everything this experience brought I'm really grateful I got to be a part of it Thank

